WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:06.912
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:06.912 --> 00:00:10.040
So again, we have now
all tools in place

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:13.180
to do a next round of
cross-section calculations.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:16.170
We have seen how to set
up a matrix element.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:20.100
We have seen how to build spin
average or to treat the spin,

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.010
and then specifically
to calculate

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:26.960
spin average amplitudes
using [INAUDIBLE]..

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:30.620
All right, I'm not saying
that this is all easy now,

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:33.020
but you have seen
all necessarily

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:37.410
elements to calculate a
cross-section for QED process.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:38.630
So let's summarize.

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:45.730
So we have seen that we can set
up some matrix element using

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:47.780
Feynman rules for QED.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:53.970
We have seen how to set up the
spin average matrix element

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:55.890
squared using the traces.

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:58.590
Now we would have to
evaluate the traces in order

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:02.140
to derive this formula here.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:05.550
So I'll spare you a precise
discussion of this step here,

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:11.730
but you can actually follow
this quite straightforwardly.

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:14.370
Let me just step back a
little bit before we proceed.

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:17.220
My goal for the class is
not to have you calculate

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:20.340
all kinds of
cross-section processes,

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:23.220
but to understand
how you would do it,

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.440
for the purpose of
really understanding

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:29.340
where dependencies come from and
where this kind of calculation

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.000
has its limitations.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.660
The first part is
you want to see

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:38.420
what is the dependencies
on the couplings involved.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:40.440
You see this g
squared, for example.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.300
That's a rather
important effect.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:44.910
You also want to see, so
Fermi's this golden rule,

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:48.210
how we get actually into the
cross-section from the matrix

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:50.500
element calculation.

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:52.860
So if you ever had to
calculate a matrix element,

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:56.640
am I going to ask you to
do this once, maybe twice,

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:59.190
as part of the homework set.

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:02.880
I encourage you to open
the book, follow the rules,

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.200
look up tricks, how
to work with traces.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.690
And then you should get
to a reasonable solution

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.009
in a reasonable amount of time.

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:16.430
But here for the purpose
of this discussion,

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:19.580
we want to just have a look
at a few specific cases

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:21.890
where we make assumptions
and simplifications

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:23.520
to the discussion.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:26.850
So the first one is
called Mott scattering.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:30.822
So here, again, we are at this
example of a spin-half particle

00:02:30.822 --> 00:02:32.405
scattering with a
spin-half particle--

00:02:32.405 --> 00:02:36.710
a different spin-half particle,
so an exchange of a photon.

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:39.890
So we used the example of
an electron-muon scattering,

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:43.160
but this muon here could also
be a proton or any other nuclei

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:44.930
we spin off.

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:48.710
The assumption for Mott
scattering we are using

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:51.260
is that the mass of
this particle, the muon,

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:53.870
is much heavier than the
mass of the electron.

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:58.970
And that's true the muon 200
times heavier than an electron.

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:00.800
A proton is even heavier.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:04.145
Any heavier nuclei of this
feels even heavier than this.

00:03:04.145 --> 00:03:06.590
In Mott scattering, we
also make the assumption

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:08.570
that the momenta
involved are lower

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:10.310
than the mass of
the heavy particle

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:15.080
and that the recoil of the
heavy nuclei, or the muon,

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:17.300
can be neglected.

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:23.090
If we do that, we can then write
the differential cross-section

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:26.270
using Fermi's golden rule
as a spin average matrix

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:29.920
element squared divided
by 2 pi M squared.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:31.490
OK.

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:36.840
If you then use this
kinematic information,

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:39.690
you basically start from
this matrix element here.

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:43.020
And then you use those
vectors, those four vectors,

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:45.850
for your momentum of the
first, second, third,

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:48.240
and fourth particle.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:51.750
You find that many of the
vectors are simplifying to ME.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:53.970
So p2 times p3 is ME.

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:55.650
And so are many of the others.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:57.390
And there is a few
important factors.

00:03:57.390 --> 00:04:02.790
For example, p1 minus p3
squared is minus 4p squared sine

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:04.500
squared theta half.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:08.250
And similarly, p1 times p3.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:10.800
So you put this all in--

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.900
again, starting from
this very formula we

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.180
just had discussed before--

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:17.220
and you put all
the simplifications

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:21.300
and you get this matrix
element, which already that

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:22.790
looks much more manageable.

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:24.540
There's an M squared,
there's a p squared,

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:27.960
there's a cosine
squared theta half term,

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:32.670
and some factor which depends
on the moment times the mass.

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:35.070
And if you then add this
to Fermi's golden rule,

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:37.770
you find this equation
for your Mott scattering.

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:40.590
Again, this is the
scattering of two

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:44.040
different spin-half particles
where one is much heavier.

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:45.730
The outgoing momenta are small.

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:50.070
And the recoil of the heavier
particles can be neglected.

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:51.960
So this Mott's
formula describes,

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:54.570
for example, the Coulomb
scattering, so the scattering

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:59.080
this photon on the electric
charge of a nuclei.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:02.260
And the scattering particle
is not too heavy and not too

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:05.320
energetic, like an electron.

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:07.910
We also assume that everything
involved here is point-like.

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:13.390
We haven't had any discussion
on the charge distribution

00:05:13.390 --> 00:05:14.740
of the nuclei or anything.

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:18.370
We assume that this is
a point-like particle.

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:21.370
OK, we can further
discuss now the case

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:24.460
where the initial state
particles are non-relativistic.

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:26.980
So here our momentum
formulas simplify.

00:05:26.980 --> 00:05:31.480
This is simply M squared,
p amplitudes is 2ME.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:34.930
And alpha is q1 times q2.

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:39.520
Those are the electric charges.

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:41.860
And so then our
differential cross-section

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:44.980
further simplifies to
something you've already seen.

00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:48.490
The Lorentzian cross-section
is equal to q1 times

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:51.650
q2 divided by 4
times the energy sine

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:53.080
squared theta half squared.

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:57.730
And we have seen that as already
the Rutherford scattering

00:05:57.730 --> 00:06:00.310
cross-section when we discussed
cross-section measurements

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:02.840
in a geometrical kind of thing.

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:07.660
So this closes a loop here in
our cross-section discussion

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:10.250
how we can think
about those things.

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:12.160
The Rutherford cross-section
is nothing else

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:17.140
but a big billiard ball being
hit by a small billiard ball

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:21.820
and looking at how the
cross-section differentially

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:26.000
kind of evolves out this setup.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:29.600
All right, in this sequence
we have a little bit more

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:30.600
of a discussion.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:33.710
What happens now if we
induce higher-order terms

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:36.830
and how can we think
about those solutions?

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:39.515
And then have two extra
lectures and where

00:06:39.515 --> 00:06:42.680
we go back and
discuss spin, and also

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:47.140
how we can actually understand
this in a Lagrangian setup.

